<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>S=
pijker71:
does the managed identity also be registratd as enterprise application?</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>&=
nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>G=
itHub
Copilot: Yes, when you create a **user-assigned managed identity** in Azure=
, it
is automatically registered as an **Enterprise Application** (service
principal) in Azure Active Directory. This allows the managed identity to be
assigned permissions (such as access to Key Vault) and to be referenced in
federated credentials for Azure Workload Identity.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>&=
nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>*=
*Summary:**</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 Every
user-assigned managed identity has a corresponding Enterprise Application
(service principal) in Azure AD.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 You do
not need to register it manually; Azure handles this automatically.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 You use
the client ID or object ID of this managed identity/service principal when
configuring access and federated credentials.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>&=
nbsp;</p>

