<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>W=
hen this
exception is thrown inside a **Durable Functions activity** (orchestrator or
activity function), the Durable Task framework tries to serialize the excep=
tion
to store it in the orchestration history and/or propagate it to the
orchestrator. If your custom exception (or any inner exception) is **not
serializable** (e.g., it doesn't have a parameterless constructor, or conta=
ins
unserializable members), the Durable Task framework will throw a
`DurableSerializationException`.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>&=
nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>*=
*Why
does this happen?**</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 Durable
Functions must serialize all exceptions that cross function boundaries.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 If your
exception (or any property on it) cannot be serialized (e.g., it contains a
non-serializable object, or is missing a parameterless constructor), Durable
will throw a `DurableSerializationException` instead.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>&=
nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>*=
*How to
fix:**</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
