itHub
Copilot: No, for **Azure Workload Identity** with KEDA, the managed identity
does **not** need to be assigned to the AKS node pool itself.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span></p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>I=
nstead,
it must be assigned to the **Kubernetes workload** (via ServiceAccount
annotation) and the AKS cluster must be configured for Azure Workload Ident=
ity.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>&=
nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>*=
*Checklist
for correct setup:**</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 The
managed identity is referenced in your ServiceAccount annotation
(`azure.workload.identity/client-id`).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 The
ServiceAccount is used by your deployment/pod.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 The AKS
cluster has Azure Workload Identity enabled.</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>-=
 The
managed identity has the required Azure RBAC permissions (e.g., Service Bus
Data Receiver).</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>&=
nbsp;</p>

<p style=3D'margin:0in;font-family:Verdana;font-size:10.0pt' lang=3Den-US>*=
*Summary:**<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=C2=A0 </span></p>

